SQL statements executed within transaction blocks work with Amazon Redshift and the RPostgreSQL DBI-driver package, but are failing with RPostgres as the driver:
```R                                                                                                                       
library(DBI)                                                                                                           
conn1 <- dbConnect(RPostgreSQL::PostgreSQL(), redacted, connection, args)
conn2 <- dbConnect(RPostgres::Postgres(), redacted, connection, args)    
                                                                                                                       
f1 <- function(conn) {                                                                                                 
on.exit(dbRollback(conn), TRUE)                                                                                        
dbBegin(conn)                                                                                                          
dbGetQuery(conn, "SELECT 1")                                                                                           
}                                                                                                                      
                                                                                                                       
f2 <- function(conn) {                                                                                                 
on.exit(dbExecute(conn, "ROLLBACK"))                                                                                   
dbExecute(conn, "BEGIN")                                                                                               
dbGetQuery(conn, "SELECT 1")                                                                                           
}                                                                                                                      
                                                                                                                       
## make sure stand-alone statements work with both drivers:                                                            
dbGetQuery(conn1, "SELECT 1")                                                                                          
#>   ?column?
#> 1        1
dbGetQuery(conn2, "SELECT 1")                                                                                          
#>   ?column?
#> 1        1
                                                                                                                       
## now test SELECT 1 within a transaction:                                                                             
f1(conn1)                                                                                                              
#>   ?column?
#> 1        1
f1(conn2)                                                                                                              
#> Error in result_create(conn@ptr, statement): Failed to prepare query: ERROR:  Query cancelled on user's request
                                                                                                                       
## what about when the transaction is created explicitly with dbExecute?:                                              
f2(conn1)                                                                                                              
#>   ?column?
#> 1        1
f2(conn2)                                                                                                              
#> Error in result_create(conn@ptr, statement): Failed to prepare query: ERROR:  Query cancelled on user's request
```
DBI/driver info:
```R
packageVersion("DBI")        
#> [1] '0.7.13'
packageVersion("RPostgres")  
#> [1] '0.1.12'
packageVersion("RPostgreSQL")
#> [1] '0.6.2'
```
I've seen this problem using both Linux and Mac clients, with these libpq versions:
 * (Amazon) Linux: libpq 9.5.8-1
 * Mac OS 10.11: libpq 9.6.5

I've written [this basic C program](https://gist.github.com/mmuurr/f909ec92182c48e1c026f8901678b13e) to test prepared statements within transactions. The relevant bits are:
```c
  // BEGIN
  res = PQexec(conn, "BEGIN");
  if(PQresultStatus(res) != PGRES_COMMAND_OK) {
    fprintf(stderr, "%s", PQerrorMessage(conn));
    PQclear(res);
    error_exit(conn);
  } else {
    PQclear(res);
  }

  // SELECT (within transaction block)
  res = PQprepare(conn, "", "SELECT 1", 0, NULL);
  if(PQresultStatus(res) != PGRES_COMMAND_OK) {
    fprintf(stderr, "%s", PQerrorMessage(conn));
    PQclear(res);
    error_exit(conn);
  } else {
    fprintf(stdout, "PQresultStatus = %s\n", PQresStatus(PQresultStatus(res)));
    fprintf(stdout, "PQcmdStatus = %s\n", PQcmdStatus(res));
    fprintf(stdout, "PQcmdTuples = %s\n", PQcmdTuples(res));
    PQclear(res);
  }

  res = PQexec(conn, "ROLLBACK");
  PQclear(res);
```
Running the program against both Redshift and PostgreSQL connections yield the same (OK) results:
```bash
PQprotocolVersion = 3
PQresultStatus = PGRES_COMMAND_OK
PQcmdStatus =
PQcmdTuples =
```

Let me know how I can help testing, as I realize not everyone has (the luxury of having :-) an Amazon Redshift cluster available for use.

